WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.800
This video is sponsored by Henson Shaving.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.280
What do Ikea founder Ingvar Kamprad

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.640
Sir Richard Branson

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.880
Billionaire investor Charles Schwab

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:09.840
Walt Disney

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.000
And Henry Ford have in common?

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.120
All are or were successful entrepreneurs&nbsp;
with dyslexia – a learning disorder that

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.240
makes it very difficult to read, write, and spell.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.360
People with dyslexia have trouble&nbsp;
connecting the letters they see

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.200
to the sounds they make and as a&nbsp;
result, often struggle in school.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.560
But in the real world, they thrive. They're behind&nbsp;
some of the most successful companies on Earth.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.080
Richard Branson is one of the&nbsp;
greatest entrepreneurs of our time.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.680
He created numerous businesses under the Virgin&nbsp;
umbrella and has a net worth of $5 billion.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.280
Virgin which now employs 90,000 people would not&nbsp;
have been successful if I hadn’t been dyslexic.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.720
If you had told Branson’s classmates&nbsp;
that he’d achieve this stunning level

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.520
of success in life - they&nbsp;
may have been dumbfounded.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.200
Branson was seen as “the dumbest&nbsp;
person in school” - in his words.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.640
He had a hard time keeping up with&nbsp;
his peers because of his dyslexia

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.120
and dropped out at the age of 16.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.640
Yet he transformed his weakness into an advantage.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.120
Branson credits dyslexia with his success

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.000
because he adapted his management style to&nbsp;
suit it which he feels made him a better boss.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.120
He understood the importance&nbsp;
of communicating well.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.640
He’s famous for being an inspirational&nbsp;
leader who engages with people.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.960
And his dyslexia also forced him to

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.160
delegate tasks to those who excelled&nbsp;
in the areas where he was weak.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.240
I had to surround myself with great&nbsp;
people. Because I wasn’t good at

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.520
maths or something, I had to&nbsp;
find people who were good at it.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.280
One example is when he hired an accountant&nbsp;
during the early days of Virgin.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.160
He credits that with getting&nbsp;
the company off the ground.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.800
Branson always struggled with numbers and words.&nbsp;
Once, when he was in a board meeting at the age of

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.600
50, he couldn’t for the life of him understand&nbsp;
the difference between net and gross profit.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.720
Another person in the room kindly took him aside&nbsp;
and explained it to him with an illustration.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.080
He drew the ocean. And put a net&nbsp;
in the ocean with fish in it.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.880
He explained that the fish in&nbsp;
the net were the net profits,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.680
and the rest of the ocean was the gross turnover.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.240
And then everything became clear to Branson.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.400
Delegating tasks to the right people&nbsp;
is essential to growing any business.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:15.440
Yet many find it difficult to give up control.&nbsp;
Everyone knows of a boss who’s a micromanager!

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.360
Research has found people with dyslexia&nbsp;
are more likely to delegate authority

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.880
and also twice as likely to&nbsp;
own two or more businesses.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.000
That study out of Cass Business&nbsp;
School in London found more than

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.840
a third of American entrepreneurs&nbsp;
surveyed showed signs of dyslexia.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.120
Another commissioned by the BBC found 40% of that

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.360
country’s self-made millions showed&nbsp;
some signs of the learning disability.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.960
That's significantly higher than&nbsp;
the estimated 5% to 10% of the

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.480
general population with some degree of dyslexia.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.360
The question is: Are their entrepreneurial&nbsp;
strengths a result of nature or nurture?

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.120
A dyslexia expert believes they are&nbsp;
naturally better at certain things,

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.880
like picking up social cues.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.160
In an article in the Sacramento Bee about&nbsp;
California governor Gavin Newsom who has dyslexia,

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.760
Dr. Marilu Gorno-Tempini of UC San Francisco said:

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.280
“He can understand what people need&nbsp;
and their emotions more than others.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:12.480
I don’t think that’s a compensation. I think&nbsp;
that’s the superpower of his dyslexic brain.”

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.120
She and other scientists at UC San Francisco&nbsp;
recently discovered children with the disorder

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.960
had greater changes in heart rate and&nbsp;
facial behavior when they viewed video

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.520
clips of others experiencing emotions&nbsp;
compared to children without dyslexia.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.080
Eleanor Palser, one of the researchers who&nbsp;
conducted the study, concluded to Newsthink:

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:35.360
“This tells us that dyslexia might be associated&nbsp;
with biological differences in empathy…”

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.160
Being able to see things from&nbsp;
the other person's perspective

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.040
is extremely important for any entrepreneur.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.880
Walt Disney, who was diagnosed with&nbsp;
dyslexia, could read people very well

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:48.240
and was extremely empathetic&nbsp;
according to those who knew him.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:54.080
He dreamt up a world that children and adults&nbsp;
could enjoy and transformed Disney into an empire.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.160
Turns out, the brains of people&nbsp;
with dyslexia are wired differently.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.840
University of Cambridge researchers&nbsp;
used artificial intelligence to map

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.480
out the brain differences of hundreds of children.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.800
They discovered those with dyslexia had&nbsp;
poorly connected “hubs” within the brain

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.360
which they attributed to&nbsp;
their learning difficulties.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.240
You can liken these hubs to a train station&nbsp;
with few or poorly organized connections.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.200
So people with dyslexia literally have a&nbsp;
different way of thinking and seeing the world.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.040
Their unique brains are believed&nbsp;
to make them brilliant spies.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.600
In fact, some intelligence agencies are&nbsp;
actively recruiting people with dyslexia,

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.800
such as the UK’s GCHQ.
The agency calls their

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.720
thinking skills “mission critical” because&nbsp;
they’re great at spotting patterns that

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.040
others don’t see - crucial in trying to&nbsp;
prevent acts of terrorism for example.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.880
And increasingly important with&nbsp;
warfare moving into cyberspace.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.960
As AI and machine learning automate&nbsp;
routine tasks, creative thinking

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.640
skills could be in higher demand.
And their creativity apparently

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:58.240
leads to original ideas that translate into&nbsp;
million-dollar or billion-dollar success.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:04.000
No matter what business you’re in, the goal&nbsp;
is to make the product as good as possible.

